.TH CP 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
cp \- copy files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cp
[\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-R\fR] [\fB\-i\fR] [\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fISOURCE\fR... \fIDEST\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Copy SOURCE to DEST, or multiple SOURCE(s) into an existing directory DEST. Permission bits are always preserved (that is \fBstd::fs::copy\fR's own behavior); \fB\-p\fR additionally preserves each copy's modification time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r, \-R, \-\-recursive
Copy directories recursively.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-interactive
Prompt before overwriting an existing destination.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-preserve
Preserve modification time in addition to mode bits.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR. Commands that take file/path arguments also accept \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options, so a file literally named e.g. \fB\-oddfile\fR can still be referenced.
.SH NOTES
With \fB\-r\fR, nested subdirectory mtimes are not individually preserved even under \fB\-p\fR -- a known limitation, see mitos-utils-compat(7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a general failure (a listed file could not be processed, etc.), 2 on a usage error (missing or invalid arguments).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
